WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:02.770
All hell had broken loose.

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:06.480
Sophie could barely believe that she had survived
for this long.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.150
Getting used to one dramatic change in her
life was hard enough, but almost immediately

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:13.790
after, the entire world had seemed to go crazy.

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:19.760
As a result, she now had a whole new existence
to try and adapt to, scavenging for what little

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:23.510
scraps of food she could find in the ruined,
desolate city.

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:28.440
The monsters could appear at any time, day
or night, either one on their own or whole

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:33.079
droves of the nightmarish creatures that had
been let loose on the world.

00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:38.270
This life was somehow far more dangerous on
a day-to-day basis than her old one had been…

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:44.110
and given Sophie used to work for the SCP
Foundation, that really was saying something.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:48.510
Formerly known as Doctor Cartwright, she had
been a researcher at the Foundation for a

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:49.510
number of years.

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.530
While she had been far from reaching the upper
echelons of the organization’s most notorious

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:58.450
staff – with the likes of Doctors Jack Bright
and Kain Pathos Crow – nobody could deny

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:02.040
Sophie was an expert when it came to studying
the anomalous.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:06.789
As much as she understood the importance of
their directive to safeguard humanity, the

00:01:06.789 --> 00:01:10.310
Foundation had, first and foremost, been a
job to her.

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:14.609
A means to an end, something to earn a living
from and then move on.

00:01:14.609 --> 00:01:19.570
It wasn’t worth giving up everything for,
and when her life needed more focus than her

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:23.039
work, Sophie wasn’t hesitant to step away.

00:01:23.039 --> 00:01:28.439
Her mother had fallen ill, terminally, and
as a result, needed around-the-clock care.

00:01:28.439 --> 00:01:33.630
So, Doctor Sophie Cartwright had no choice
but to hand in her resignation from the SCP

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:34.630
Foundation.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:39.639
There were times when she missed it, and moments
after her mother eventually passed that Sophie

00:01:39.639 --> 00:01:41.270
considered going back.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:46.090
But she came to realize it wasn’t the work
itself she missed, not even her co-workers

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:47.560
at the Foundation.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:53.289
It was the occasional, rare anomaly that was
just a completely harmless little oddity,

00:01:53.289 --> 00:01:55.810
not a monstrous abomination.

00:01:55.810 --> 00:02:01.090
Often in her new, unfamiliar life of resignation,
Sophie would find herself looking wistfully

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:04.209
at a picture of herself and one such anomaly.

00:02:04.209 --> 00:02:08.390
Technically, it was against the Foundation’s
rules to possess any photographic evidence

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:13.780
of SCPs, but Sophie had made sure it didn’t
look like anything out of the ordinary.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:18.410
The picture had been taken on her phone, and
showed Sophie crouched down, with the face

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:23.370
of a curious, but completely normal-looking
black and grey tabby cat poking up in the

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.370
foreground.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:29.860
Nothing about the picture gave any indication
that the cat, named Josie, was actually missing

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.810
her hindquarters and back legs.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:38.440
SCP-529, as the Foundation had designated
her, was one of the few completely harmless

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:44.730
SCPs in their care, her only anomalous trait
being that her entire back half was missing;

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:50.190
either because it didn’t exist or had somehow
been displaced in another dimension; hence

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:53.140
her nickname, Josie the Half Cat.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:57.170
Given that she had been allowed to roam freely
around the Foundation facility she was kept

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:03.140
in, Josie had run across Doctor Cartwright
a number of times, the pair of them even becoming

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:04.300
friends.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:10.140
With a sigh and a smile, Sophie had been looking
at the photo on the day everything went wrong.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:14.190
Steel-capped boots came rushing up the stairs
and down the corridor to her apartment, worn

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:16.520
by a group of black-clad mercenaries.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:21.240
You see, unbeknownst to Sophie, at the time
she had tendered her resignation from the

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:26.510
SCP Foundation, a number of the organization’s
higher-ups had also done the same.

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:30.970
While Sophie’s reasons for leaving were
purely personal, the wave of resignations

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:35.940
from other high-ranking Foundation personnel
came as a reaction to something called Project

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:36.960
PNEUMA.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:41.850
This was an operation that had uncovered a
disturbing truth about the psychosphere – a

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:47.160
shared human unconsciousness – that had
led to the O5 Council proposing a new directive

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.050
for the organization.

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:51.340
Those that objected left the Foundation.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:57.150
Others were killed by their former employer
and its merciless hit squads in cold blood.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:04:01.421
Perhaps thanks to some administrative error
filing Sophie’s resignation alongside those

00:04:01.421 --> 00:04:05.591
that had left after learning about PNEUMA,
or maybe because the Foundation didn’t want

00:04:05.591 --> 00:04:10.120
her extensive knowledge of the anomalous being
used against their newfound directive, they

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:13.430
had sent a Mobile Task Force to her apartment.

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:17.729
Filing down the corridor, the soldiers lifted
a battering ram and swung it forward, shattering

00:04:17.729 --> 00:04:22.009
the door, wooden splinters littering the floor
as they breached the entrance.

00:04:22.009 --> 00:04:26.979
But Sophie had heard them coming, escaping
as fast as she could, descending a fire escape

00:04:26.979 --> 00:04:29.419
ladder until she reached the street below.

00:04:29.419 --> 00:04:33.719
At that moment in time, Sophie had no idea
why the Foundation had sent a hit squad out

00:04:33.719 --> 00:04:34.719
to kill her.

00:04:34.719 --> 00:04:37.759
Of course, the answer to that question quickly
became obvious.

00:04:37.759 --> 00:04:42.599
Shortly after, reports came pouring in from
all over the world, broadcast over every news

00:04:42.599 --> 00:04:45.199
channel before they were ultimately blocked.

00:04:45.199 --> 00:04:51.499
The SCP Foundation had gone public, revealing
its existence to the entire world, and declaring

00:04:51.499 --> 00:04:53.430
its new mission statement.

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:58.689
Rather than protecting the world from anomalies,
the Foundation had declared war, calling for

00:04:58.689 --> 00:05:01.759
the total and utter extinction of humanity.

00:05:01.759 --> 00:05:06.919
While Sophie had no way of knowing the reasons
for the Foundation going rogue, it was clear

00:05:06.919 --> 00:05:10.960
that working for them in the past had put
a target on her back.

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:15.750
Luckily, she managed to avoid the MTF coming
after her, getting swept up in a crowd that

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:22.159
was fleeing from something far more horrific:
SCP-682 had been released, and was devouring

00:05:22.159 --> 00:05:24.569
people in broad daylight.

00:05:24.569 --> 00:05:27.139
The waking nightmare didn’t stop there.

00:05:27.139 --> 00:05:32.319
Over the next few months, the Foundation released
a number of dangerous anomalies on the world,

00:05:32.319 --> 00:05:37.409
using them to slaughter innocent people in
droves, assassinate world leaders and cripple

00:05:37.409 --> 00:05:42.370
organizations like the Global Occult Coalition
to hinder humanity’s chances of fighting

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:43.370
back.

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:48.659
And in amongst it all, living through the
chaos and carnage were tiny pockets of survivors.

00:05:48.659 --> 00:05:53.070
Although those too were starting to be rapidly
picked off by the Foundation, deploying more

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:58.349
of their Mobile Task Force operatives or compliant
SCPs to eliminate the refugees.

00:05:58.349 --> 00:06:03.180
Sophie had witnessed firsthand the Foundation
attacks, and as a result, had found it best

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:07.949
to keep moving from camp to camp, only staying
with one group of fellow survivors for a few

00:06:07.949 --> 00:06:10.469
nights each before moving on.

00:06:10.469 --> 00:06:14.830
She wasn’t sure if the Foundation was specifically
attacking the refugee camps she stopped at,

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:18.930
or if it was a continuous, systematic extermination
effort.

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:23.029
She’d been noting down her ponderings in
a half-burned notebook she had found while

00:06:23.029 --> 00:06:28.059
scavenging abandoned grocery stores for any
remaining food that was still edible.

00:06:28.059 --> 00:06:32.999
A cynical part of her thought it was perhaps
pointless to document what was happening,

00:06:32.999 --> 00:06:37.219
but the former Researcher in Sophie wanted
to preserve any information she could about

00:06:37.219 --> 00:06:40.360
what the Foundation was subjecting people
to.

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:45.030
If anything, that knowledge might prove useful
to someone in the future…

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:46.680
although ‘what future?’

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:52.259
was a question Sophie Cartwright kept returning
to, with no optimistic answer in sight.

00:06:52.259 --> 00:06:57.990
It was easy to miss how things had once been,
to yearn for life to go back the way it once

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:04.120
was, even though that was impossible now that
anomalies were rampaging the world over.

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:11.289
It made sleeping hard, the fear of waking
up to see the face of SCP-682 or SCP-096 staring

00:07:11.289 --> 00:07:14.089
back at her the moment she opened her eyes.

00:07:14.089 --> 00:07:19.789
One night, it was worse than ever; every sound,
no matter how faint or far away, in Sophie’s

00:07:19.789 --> 00:07:24.219
mind, could have been made by a carnivorous
creature, or an approaching Foundation foot

00:07:24.219 --> 00:07:25.219
soldier.

00:07:25.219 --> 00:07:29.150
A bottle clinked, waking Sophie from her restless
sleep.

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:34.419
Something was nearby, and from the sound of
it, getting closer and closer.

00:07:34.419 --> 00:07:38.309
Sitting up in the alleyway she’d been sleeping
in, Sophie looked around her, feeling her

00:07:38.309 --> 00:07:40.800
heart thumping against her ribs in fear.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:46.379
Then, through the dark, she caught the sight
of a pair of beady, reflective eyes, followed

00:07:46.379 --> 00:07:48.770
by the sounds of a drawn-out, defensive yowl.

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:53.050
For a second, she wondered if it could be…
no, surely not?

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:56.930
The eyes approached, a small, shadowy form
behind them.

00:07:56.930 --> 00:08:01.360
Sophie could barely make it out in the night’s
darkness, even with the glow of distant fires

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:02.860
in the sky on the horizon.

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:08.210
But from what she could see, the creature
approaching her was small, just about smaller

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:11.740
than an average cat; about half the size.

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:16.590
Her fingers trembling, she reached out a hand
towards it as gently as she could, allowing

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:18.360
it to sniff her for a moment.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:23.400
Then, she felt it duck under her open palm,
letting her stroke up its arched back.

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:28.689
When her hand reached about halfway, Sophie
could feel the slight yield of a barely tangible

00:08:28.689 --> 00:08:32.099
back half, which could only mean one thing…

00:08:32.099 --> 00:08:33.099
“Josie?”

00:08:33.099 --> 00:08:36.140
Sophie asked, holding a sob at the back of
her throat.

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:41.850
The little half-cat replied with a meow, recognizing
the voice of her human friend from the Foundation.

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:47.130
Immediately, she leaped into the former researcher’s
lap, allowing Doctor Cartwright to hold her

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:48.450
and stroke her.

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:53.580
Giving in, so glad to be reunited with Josie,
Sophie started to let out stifled sobbing

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:58.810
sounds – careful not to attract attention,
but allowing herself to be the happiest she’d

00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:01.930
felt in the months since this had all started.

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:06.330
Although she had no way of understanding Josie’s
meows, Doctor Cartwright assumed that the

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:11.470
brave little Half Cat had managed to escape
the Foundation facility she used to call home

00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:13.480
with relative ease.

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:17.820
And she was right; since Josie was allowed
to roam freely, she had been able to make

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:22.880
a break for it when the Foundation’s MTF
kill squads opened fire on their own personnel

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:25.550
at their various sites across the globe.

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:30.330
The loud noise had caused Josie the Half Cat
to flee and hide, until the sounds of gunfire

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:32.650
and screams had died down.

00:09:32.650 --> 00:09:37.470
By the time she emerged from her hiding place,
a number of the anomalies once kept in containment

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:39.930
had now been set loose on the world.

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:46.420
With the facility busted wide open, SCP-529
had taken it upon herself to leave, and had

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:52.340
been surviving in much the same way Sophie
had; scavenging for scraps, passing from one

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:57.870
refugee camp to the next, usually greeted
with curiosity by any ordinary civilian who

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:01.500
had never seen a cat with its entire back
half missing.

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:06.620
Now that they had been tearfully reunited,
Josie and Sophie stuck together on their continuing

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:09.070
travels through the now treacherous and SCP-infested
world.

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:14.900
Sophie’s entries in her makeshift journal
started to take on a much more positive tone;

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:20.430
while it was hard to be entirely optimistic
living through an anomalous apocalypse, her

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:25.680
spirits were at least lifted by having her
little Half Cat companion alongside her.

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:31.310
Josie never seemed to stray too far from Sophie’s
side as they hiked their way across the country.

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:36.700
Occasionally, SCP-529 would climb up her human
friend’s back, and rest on her shoulder

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:41.591
as they traversed more open terrain, her missing
back half making it look like the little Half

00:10:41.591 --> 00:10:46.490
Cat was perched precariously on her front
two legs next to Sophie’s head.

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:50.570
And, of course, whenever they stopped for
the night, Josie would sleep curled up and

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:54.050
purring contentedly, right next to Sophie.

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:59.081
When the morning came, she would awaken, stretch
out her front paws with a yawn, then nuzzle

00:10:59.081 --> 00:11:02.110
at Sophie’s sleeping face to wake her up.

00:11:02.110 --> 00:11:06.880
At first, Doctor Cartwright’s plan had been
to head due West from where she had started,

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:10.500
hoping to reach Yellowstone National Park
and access SCP-2000.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:15.580
There was a Foundation facility there, hidden
deep underground and filled with all manner

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:17.640
of anomalous technology.

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:22.430
It was all intended to be used in case the
world ended, and the human race was wiped

00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:27.710
out, even capable of mass producing new human
beings with customized memories, as well as

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:31.910
holding a backup of all cumulative knowledge
and data on Earth.

00:11:31.910 --> 00:11:36.610
Every book ever written, every terabyte of
information on the Internet, everything.

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:41.140
However, passing through another camp, Sophie
and Josie received word from one of the other

00:11:41.140 --> 00:11:42.140
survivors.

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:46.700
Apparently, the Foundation had predicted that
somebody with knowledge of SCP-2000 might

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:51.540
try something like that and how it would undermine
their plans to wipe out humanity.

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:55.750
During one of the moments wherein rebelling
groups were able to temporarily restore TV

00:11:55.750 --> 00:12:00.190
and Internet service, it was reported that
the Foundation had already put a stop to that

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:02.620
potential option for averting their apocalypse.

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:07.990
They had intentionally triggered an eruption
of the Yellowstone super volcano, totally

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:15.120
obliterating the facility housing SCP-2000
and one of humanity’s last hopes at survival.

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:17.830
“Well, thanks for telling me,” Sophie
sighed.

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:23.100
By now, she had long given up almost all hope
that things would ever be the same again.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:27.630
But hearing the news about Yellowstone had
extinguished the last little ember of optimism

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:29.000
that she had left.

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:30.840
There was no way back.

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:35.700
Sensing her friend was upset, Josie meowed
at her, either as if to tell Sophie ‘It’s

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:37.740
okay’ or ‘I’m hungry’.

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:41.600
As the pair turned to leave though, the other
survivor called out to them.

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:42.850
“Wait!” they said.

00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:44.430
“Look, I heard another rumor.

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:49.130
And it might be a total myth, but there’s
meant to be a sanctuary not far from here.”

00:12:49.130 --> 00:12:51.190
“What do you mean ‘sanctuary’?”

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:52.190
Sophie asked.

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:53.740
“Like another camp?

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:55.880
The Foundation will wipe it out within a week.”

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:58.450
“No, not a camp,” the survivor replied.

00:12:58.450 --> 00:13:03.260
“It’s just a rumor, but supposedly there’s
a refuge, some place where the SCP Foundation

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:04.380
can’t get to.

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:07.630
Apparently, there’s shelter, as well as
plenty of food and water, probably enough

00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:09.500
for you and the little one.”

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:13.400
Sophie looked out of the corner of her eye
at Josie, balanced on her shoulder.

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:17.610
The pair of them had gone weeks without eating
before arriving at this camp, and she had

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:21.240
no idea how long it would be before they reached
the next.

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:26.490
The sight of her Half Cat companion motivated
her; they needed to survive, as long as they

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:28.880
did, the Foundation couldn’t win.

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:32.550
Giving the other survivor a nod, they pointed
her in the right direction.

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:38.740
“That way, few miles at least, but you’ve
gotta get through the forest,” they explained.

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:42.400
“Be careful, the Foundation’s got people
out on patrols over there.

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:46.600
And they’re real weird too, I seen ‘em
doing this strange ritual, letting their commander

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:49.910
stab them, testing whether they can still
feel pain or not.”

00:13:49.910 --> 00:13:54.290
Within a few hours, the sun had set, with
both Sophie and Josie deep in the heart of

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:55.450
the woods.

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:59.720
Just like every night since the Foundation
had initiated their war on humanity, every

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:04.900
sound – every snapped twig or rustling of
leaves in the breeze – filled Sophie with

00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:05.900
dread.

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:10.960
But this time, at least she knew with certainty
that there were MTF troops nearby.

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:15.700
She could see their flashlights, beams of
light bleeding through between the trees.

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:20.260
Carefully, holding Josie in her arms, she
stepped carefully and as quietly as she could

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:21.490
through the forest.

00:14:21.490 --> 00:14:26.280
The Half Cat was trembling, clearly feeling
just as fearful as Sophie was.

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:30.230
The former researcher was praying that her
little friend wouldn’t make a sound; she

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:34.750
knew she wouldn’t be able to help it if
she did, cats did instinctively make defensive

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:36.440
yowls if they felt threatened.

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:40.990
But even the slightest noise from Josie would
give away their position to the MTF sweeping

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:41.990
the area.

00:14:41.990 --> 00:14:42.990
“Fan out!”

00:14:42.990 --> 00:14:45.200
she heard the task force’s field commander
call.

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:48.800
“Even spread, if there’s anyone here,
you all know what to do.”

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:53.610
Sophie ducked down behind a fallen tree, covering
her own mouth with her free hand, trying to

00:14:53.610 --> 00:14:55.120
stop her panicked breathing.

00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:59.420
Her only options were to make a break for
it, dash through the forest, and hope she

00:14:59.420 --> 00:15:03.029
and Josie weren’t caught… or worse, killed.

00:15:03.029 --> 00:15:06.610
Though these days, there was really no difference
between the two.

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:10.620
Or she could wait for the MTF to pass by,
continuing their search.

00:15:10.620 --> 00:15:15.570
She had spent months trying to survive in
the anomalous, apocalyptic nightmare, by now,

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:18.340
the Foundation couldn’t have known she was
there.

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:23.000
Their forces were just combing the forest
looking for any survivors they could find.

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:26.880
But if she stayed still, there was still a
chance one of the MTF would find her, and

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:31.350
Josie, and would either alert the others,
or just open fire on the spot.

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:34.830
All things considered, neither option sounded
ideal.

00:15:34.830 --> 00:15:37.960
Josie was getting restless, trying to wiggle
free of Sophie’s grip.

00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:42.460
The Half Cat’s incorporeal back half meant
Sophie could only hold onto what little underside

00:15:42.460 --> 00:15:43.960
her feline friend had.

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:48.280
Slowly, she started to get up, and made her
way out from behind the tree… right into

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:50.450
the path of an MTF trooper’s flashlight.

00:15:50.450 --> 00:15:53.029
“Got a live one!” he yelled.

00:15:53.029 --> 00:15:56.210
A flash of light and loud bang echoed through
the forest.

00:15:56.210 --> 00:16:00.550
The bullet had torn through Sophie’s chest,
narrowly missing her Half Cat, but the noise

00:16:00.550 --> 00:16:03.470
of the shot had been more than enough to startle
her.

00:16:03.470 --> 00:16:07.710
As Sophie dropped to the forest floor, gripping
her heavily bleeding wound and writhing in

00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:11.360
pain, Josie leaped into the air towards the
Foundation operative.

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:16.990
Either in shock, or because she somehow understood
her protector had been shot, the brave little

00:16:16.990 --> 00:16:19.770
cat started clawing at the soldier’s face.

00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:24.070
Despite her sharp front claws tearing at him,
leaving red lines over his face, the armed

00:16:24.070 --> 00:16:28.490
man didn’t react; it was as if he couldn’t
feel pain at all.

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:32.680
The MTF trooper gripped Josie by her neck
fur, pulling her away from him.

00:16:32.680 --> 00:16:37.760
Before he could reach for his sidearm, someone
charged at him, knocking the Foundation operator

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:38.760
over.

00:16:38.760 --> 00:16:41.790
Another shot rang out as he fell, tackled
by Sophie.

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:46.120
Meanwhile, Josie had dropped to the ground,
landing on her two visible feet and hissing

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:47.500
at the soldier.

00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:51.670
Scrambling to her feet, Sophie picked up Josie
and ran, the sounds of the rest of the task

00:16:51.670 --> 00:16:54.390
force receding into the distance behind her.

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:58.640
The little Half Cat was looking back over
her human friend’s shoulder, watching as

00:16:58.640 --> 00:17:03.870
the sweeping flashlights of the MTF kill squad
fanned between the trees, until they eventually

00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:05.689
disappeared from view.

00:17:05.689 --> 00:17:10.060
Her earlier adrenaline spike began to drop
sharply in time with the gradual rise of the

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:11.060
sun.

00:17:11.060 --> 00:17:14.970
Sophie had long since slowed down, certain
that she’d been able to give the Foundation’s

00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:16.820
foot soldiers the slip.

00:17:16.820 --> 00:17:21.030
Josie had taken her favorite perch on Sophie’s
shoulder again, meaning she could use her

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:23.680
hands to apply pressure to her two wounds.

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:28.000
But with every step, the former researcher
could feel her limbs getting heavier.

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:33.110
Her pulse, which had drummed faster than ever
as her and her Half Cat had fled, was now

00:17:33.110 --> 00:17:35.650
slowing down with every fading beat.

00:17:35.650 --> 00:17:39.370
She was tired, so much that she could barely
keep her head up.

00:17:39.370 --> 00:17:42.550
Until, eventually, Sophie had to stop.

00:17:42.550 --> 00:17:47.040
She sat at the edge of a lake in the middle
of the forest, exhaustedly trying to catch

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:48.040
her breath.

00:17:48.040 --> 00:17:52.700
Calmly, Josie hopped off her shoulder and
sat down at her side, licking her paw and

00:17:52.700 --> 00:17:54.820
cleaning behind her ears.

00:17:54.820 --> 00:17:59.770
Sophie looked at her little friend and smiled,
feeling a tear run down her face.

00:17:59.770 --> 00:18:03.870
She reached into her bag and produced her
journal, tearing out a page and scrawling

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:06.350
down a note with the last of her strength.

00:18:06.350 --> 00:18:09.740
“To whoever finds this Half Cat: her name
is Josie.

00:18:09.740 --> 00:18:12.230
She’s my only friend left in the whole world.

00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:16.100
I’ve taken care of her for as long as I
can, but I’ll be dead soon.

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:18.720
Please take care of her for me, take her to
safety.

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:21.410
Be good to her and she’ll be good to you.

00:18:21.410 --> 00:18:25.630
And if you scavenge any cheese, only give
her a little as a treat.”

00:18:25.630 --> 00:18:30.150
After tying the note to Josie’s midsection,
so it rested atop her semi-corporeal back

00:18:30.150 --> 00:18:33.350
half, Sophie looked around one last time.

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:40.650
The lake was calm, a tiny pocket of remaining
peace in a world gone mad, overrun with anomalies.

00:18:40.650 --> 00:18:45.610
“Guess we did make it to a sanctuary after
all, Josie,” she whispered weakly.

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:50.520
Giving her little Half Cat one last scratch
behind the ears, Josie meowed gratefully.

00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:55.860
Finally, unable to keep her eyes open and
her head up any longer, Sophie Cartwright

00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:57.670
let herself drift off.

00:18:57.670 --> 00:19:00.530
Josie was left meowing at her friend to wake
up.

00:19:00.530 --> 00:19:05.040
She sat with her all day; she didn’t move,
didn’t even wake up when Josie nuzzled her

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:07.210
face against hers like she had before.

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:12.180
Sophie’s face felt much colder now against
the confused Half Cat’s fur.

00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:16.390
Eventually, Josie wandered off into the forest,
towards an uncertain future.

00:19:16.390 --> 00:19:22.580
Maybe, if this future wasn’t set in stone,
if it could be reset, then Josie the Half

00:19:22.580 --> 00:19:25.309
Cat would see her friend Sophie again…

00:19:25.309 --> 00:19:30.780
Now go and check out “SCP-5000 THE FULL
STORY COMPILATION” if you want to know more

00:19:30.780 --> 00:19:32.710
about the events that led up now.

00:19:32.710 --> 00:19:38.340
Or, take a little trip back in time and spend
a day with Josie the Half Cat in “IS SCP-999

00:19:38.340 --> 00:19:40.050
NO LONGER THE CUTEST SCP?

00:19:40.050 --> 00:19:41.160
SCP-529 JOSIE THE HALF CAT”.

